{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And you shall be to Me, etc.</b> One needs to know why it was necessary to say ״וְאַתֶּם,״ since until now He had been speaking to them. And perhaps because the words refer back to Moses and Aaron, and since until now He had been speaking about all Israel and made them a treasured possession — if so, Moses would say that there is no distinction between them and the rest of Israel; therefore He said <b>וְאַתֶּם</b>, meaning you and your brother: <b>you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests</b> — corresponding to Aaron; <b>and a holy nation</b> — corresponding to Moses and his family, after the manner of what He said (Numbers 8:17–18): ״I consecrated them to Myself, and I took the Levites,״ etc." "<b>Or</b> it may intend to tell them that from now on they will be in the category of the supernal retinue, for on high the Master has His ministers and attendants who serve before Him in the height, and they are called priests; and He also has a great host before Him called holy, as it is written (Daniel 8:13): ״one holy one.״ And HaShem said that He will make them — in place of the supernal priests and holy ones — and so it was, that He commanded to make Him a house to dwell among them, and chose priests from among them, and commanded us (Leviticus 11:44): ״and you shall be holy.״ And according to this, His saying <b>וְאַתֶּם</b> refers back to the entirety of Israel, and it was necessary to say the word <b>וְאַתֶּם</b>, lest they think that His words to them concern the future, when the soul ascends after separation from the body and the spirit attains the arranged degrees — but while alive, its vitality distances the intellect from grasping this; therefore He said <b>וְאַתֶּם</b>, meaning: you yourselves, possessors of a body, shall attain this degree. And we find that the righteous attained this degree, being called angels and holy ones, for through the medium of Torah a person's degree is elevated beyond limit, above the angels (Sanhedrin 93a) — and this is your sign, that HaShem chose to grant greater distinction to the sanctity of His dwelling place together with Israel. From this you learn that they are above them, for one ascends in holiness, etc." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes, according to what I explained in the verse ״you shall be to Me a treasured possession,״ that it alludes to the refinement of the holy sparks. His saying <b>וְאַתֶּם</b>, etc., is to magnify the preeminence of the refiner over that which is refined, for the refiner will become a kingdom of priests and of a holy nation — these being two degrees of holiness that are refined, as mentioned. And He divided them into two parts, for among the refined there are higher degrees and lesser degrees according to the degree of holiness, and the intention in this is that they possess the quality of kingship over that which is refined. And go forth and learn from Moses: because he brought Israel out of Egypt, he acquired kingship over them." "<b>These are the words, etc.</b> The verse's intention, according to what we explained just above in the verse ״you shall be to Me״ as referring to Israel, is to be precise on this basis: that you should not think that My saying ״you shall be to Me,״ etc., refers to you, Moses and Aaron — not so, but rather ״these are the words that you shall speak to the children of Israel,״ for the prerogatives belong to them. And according to what I explained — that it refers back to Moses — the intention in His saying <b>these are the words</b>, etc., is that he shall not add to them. And if you say: it is obvious that he would not add to them, and if there is reason to suspect him — heaven forbid — why did He not command him similarly regarding the other commandments He had sent him with until now?" "<b>Rather,</b> the intention is that He commanded him not to elaborate on HaShem's words to Israel, but to say the words exactly as they are. And the Creator's reason is that since He had precision in His words — matters of fear (awe) and matters of love — as we explained them in the verse (Exodus 19:3) ״כֹּה תֹאמַר,״ the Creator was concerned lest Moses, after speaking HaShem's words to them, come to explain the things by adding exhortation of his own to instill fear in Israel's heart or to elaborate on love of the Creator. Therefore He said to him: do not speak further on this matter beyond <b>these words</b>, for if Moses speaks words of fear they will come to serve from the side of fear — but HaShem did not so counsel, and did not mention fear except like one who adds spice to a dish to give it a pleasant flavor, as I wrote there — that this is so a person should not treat any moment or any detail lightly by saying that the Beloved will not be insistent; rather, the entire essence of divine service requires love. And it turns out that Moses's words would impair the intention, heaven forbid." "Further, it appears that HaShem wished to test them, and He measured out words sufficient for them from moral instruction and from love for the purpose of receiving the Torah. And if their soul is not stirred upon hearing these words — this will indicate that they are far from purity of heart. Therefore, if Moses were to continue to speak many words, whether in the aspect of fear or in the aspect of love, even if he were to state explicitly that they are his own words — behold, he would frustrate the Creator's intention in His test; for even if they would accept, there is room to say that it was Moses' persuasiveness that brought it about. Moreover, there is in this a diminishment of the preciousness of the Torah and the glory of the house of its Father, the great El. Therefore HaShem commanded that he add nothing further to speak, not even a word from his own mouth. Understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:17.763646081Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}